## Test Anti-Pattern Audit: `ProductCatalogTests.cs`

### Summary

**10 tests analyzed → 6 Critical, 1 High, 1 Medium, 1 Low finding**

This suite is a textbook **coverage-touching** test class. Every public method on `ProductCatalog` is called (yielding ~95% line coverage), but almost nothing is actually verified. These tests will pass even if every method returns garbage or silently corrupts data.

---

### 🔴 Critical — False Confidence

#### 1. Coverage-Touching Pattern (Systemic — entire file)

The suite methodically calls all 10 public methods in declaration order. Of the 10 tests:
- **5 have zero assertions** (`AddProduct`, `UpdatePrice`, `RemoveProduct`, `GetProductCount`, `ApplyBulkDiscount`, `ImportFromCsv` — lines 17–21, 33–36, 39–42, 64–66, 69–74, 85–88)
- **4 assert only `IsNotNull`** (`GetProduct`, `SearchProducts`, `GetAllProducts`, `ExportToCsv`)
- **0 verify actual values, counts, content, or side effects**

This is exactly the pattern that produces high coverage with zero bug-detection ability.

#### 2. No Assertions — `AddProduct_Works` (line 17)

Calls `AddProduct` but never checks the product was actually stored.

**Fix:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void AddProduct_StoresProduct()
{
    var product = new Product("SKU-001", "Widget", 9.99m);
    _catalog.AddProduct(product);
    var stored = _catalog.GetProduct("SKU-001");
    Assert.AreEqual("Widget", stored?.Name);
    Assert.AreEqual(9.99m, stored?.Price);
}
```

#### 3. No Assertions — `UpdatePrice_Works` (line 33)

Calls `UpdatePrice` but never checks the price actually changed.

**Fix:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void UpdatePrice_ChangesStoredPrice()
{
    _catalog.AddProduct(new Product("SKU-001", "Widget", 9.99m));
    _catalog.UpdatePrice("SKU-001", 14.99m);
    var updated = _catalog.GetProduct("SKU-001");
    Assert.AreEqual(14.99m, updated?.Price);
}
```

#### 4. No Assertions — `RemoveProduct_Works` (line 39)

Never verifies the product was actually deleted.

**Fix:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void RemoveProduct_DeletesProduct()
{
    _catalog.AddProduct(new Product("SKU-001", "Widget", 9.99m));
    _catalog.RemoveProduct("SKU-001");
    Assert.IsNull(_catalog.GetProduct("SKU-001"));
}
```

#### 5. No Assertions — `GetProductCount_Works` (line 64)

Captures the count in a local variable and never asserts on it.

**Fix:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void GetProductCount_ReturnsCorrectCount()
